pe/text "I, II, and III only"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039650/state.json" "file_sha256" "255b6ddff5e791c558365517066b272de1f7a141d518142b7b01ba6570899cf6" "basis" 1378 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039650/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-114415" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/text "I, II, and IV only"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039650/state.json" "file_sha256" "255b6ddff5e791c558365517066b272de1f7a141d518142b7b01ba6570899cf6" "basis" 1378 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039650/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-114415" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/text "II only"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039650/state.json" "file_sha256" "255b6ddff5e791c558365517066b272de1f7a141d518142b7b01ba6570899cf6" "basis" 1378 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039650/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-114415" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/text "III only"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039650/state.json" "file_sha256" "255b6ddff5e791c558365517066b272de1f7a141d518142b7b01ba6570899cf6" "basis" 1378 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039650/edb-import/verification.json"}] "sources" [{"question" "q-2471" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "For the function $f(x)=5{x}^{3}+15{x}^{2}-10,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${f}^{″}(0)>0$\n- $x=0$ is a relative maximum of $f(x)$\n- ${f}^{″}(-2)<0$\n- $x=-2$ is a relative minimum of $f(x)$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14065668/state.json" "file_sha256" "e511109240535f5f98d64c365250a5834ddfbc9fc45b29ff3a8d5c76bc911657"} {"question" "q-2471" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We need to identify the critical points. Then, we then need to classify them using the second derivative test.\n\nFirst, we need to identify the critical points. Differentiating $f(x)=5{x}^{3}+15{x}^{2}-10,$ we get\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=15{x}^{2}+30x,\n$$\n\nand solving ${f}^{′}(x)=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}15{x}^{2}+30x & =0 \\\\ 15x(x+2) & =0 \\\\ x & =0,-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we have critical points at $x=0,-2.$\n\nNext, we need to determine the sign of ${f}^{″}(x)$ at the critical points. Computing ${f}^{″}(x),$ we get\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(x)=30x+30,\n$$\n\nand evaluating ${f}^{″}(x)$ at each critical point, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(0) & =30>0 \\\\ {f}^{″}(-2) & =-30<0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe conclude that:\n\n- Statements I is true while statement II is false. The second derivative is positive at $x=0,$ so this is a relative minimum of $f(x).$\n\n- Statements III is true while statement VI is false. The second derivative is negative at $x=-2,$ so this is a relative maximum of $f(x).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only\"." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14065668/state.json" "file_sha256" "e511109240535f5f98d64c365250a5834ddfbc9fc45b29ff3a8d5c76bc911657"} {"question" "q-2471" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14065668/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "7ad5bf91b4e12d86df59584f1d9f010aa08b83bce64bdb52579a71f9358b936e"} {"question" "q-2471" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14065668/state.json" "file_sha256" "e511109240535f5f98d64c365250a5834ddfbc9fc45b29ff3a8d5c76bc911657"} {"question" "q-2471" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I, II, III, and IV"] ["text" "II and IV only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14065668/state.json" "file_sha256" "e511109240535f5f98d64c365250a5834ddfbc9fc45b29ff3a8d5c76bc911657"} {"question" "q-2471" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "I and III only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14065668/state.json" "file_sha256" "e511109240535f5f98d64c365250a5834ddfbc9fc45b29ff3a8d5c76bc911657"} {"question" "q-21500" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "For the function $f(x)=-3{x}^{5/2}+5{x}^{3/2}+15$ on $(0,\\infty ),$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $x=1$ is a critical point of $f(x)$\n- ${f}^{″}(1)<0$\n- $f(x)$ has a relative minimum at $x=1$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14065668/state.json" "file_sha256" "e511109240535f5f98d64c365250a5834ddfbc9fc45b29ff3a8d5c76bc911657"} {"question" "q-21500" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We need to identify whether $x=1$ is a critical point. If it is, we then need to classify it using the second derivative test.\n\nFirst, we need to identify the critical points. Differentiating $f(x)=-3{x}^{5/2}+5{x}^{3/2}+15,$ we get\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=-3\\cdot \\frac{5}{2}{x}^{3/2}+5\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}{x}^{1/2}=-\\frac{15}{2}{x}^{3/2}+\\frac{15}{2}{x}^{1/2},\n$$\n\nand solving ${f}^{′}(x)=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{15}{2}{x}^{3/2}+\\frac{15}{2}{x}^{1/2} & =0 \\\\ -\\frac{15}{2}{x}^{1/2}(x-1) & =0 \\\\ x & =0,1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we have a critical point at $x=1.$\n\nNext, we need to determine the sign of ${f}^{″}(x)$ at this critical point. Computing ${f}^{″}(x),$ we get\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(x)=-\\frac{15}{2}\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}{x}^{1/2}+\\frac{15}{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}{x}^{-1/2}=-\\frac{45}{4}{x}^{1/2}+\\frac{15}{4}{x}^{-1/2},\n$$\n\nand evaluating ${f}^{″}(x)$ at $x=1$, we get\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(1)=-\\frac{45}{4}(1)^{1/2}+\\frac{15}{4}(1)^{-1/2}=-\\frac{15}{2}<0.\n$$\n\nThe second derivative is negative at $x=1,$ so this critical point is a relative *maximum*. \n\nTherefore, comparing our results with the given statements, we conclude that:\n\n- $x=1$ is a critical point of $f(x)\\,✓$\n\n- ${f}^{″}(1)<0\\,✓$\n\n- $f(x)$ has a relative *maximum* at $x=1\\,\\times $\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14065668/state.json" "file_sha256" "e511109240535f5f98d64c365250a5834ddfbc9fc45b29ff3a8d5c76bc911657"} {"question" "q-21500" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14065668/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "7ad5bf91b4e12d86df59584f1d9f010aa08b83bce64bdb52579a71f9358b936e"} {"question" "q-21500" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14065668/state.json" "file_sha256" "e511109240535f5f98d64c365250a5834ddfbc9fc45b29ff3a8d5c76bc911657"} {"question" "q-21500" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "None of the statements are true"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14065668/state.json" "file_sha256" "e511109240535f5f98d64c365250a5834ddfbc9fc45b29ff3a8d5c76bc911657"} {"question" "q-21500" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "I and II only"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14065668/state.json" "file_sha256" "e511109240535f5f98d64c365250a5834ddfbc9fc45b29ff3a8d5c76bc911657"} {"question" "q-21521" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the function $f(x)=-\\frac{{x}^{3}}{2}+24x-36.$ Given that ${f}^{′}(4)=0,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${f}^{″}(4)>0$\n- $f(x)$ has a relative minimum at $x=4$\n- $f(x)$ has a relative maximum at $x=4$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14065668/state.json" "file_sha256" "e511109240535f5f98d64c365250a5834ddfbc9fc45b29ff3a8d5c76bc911657"} {"question" "q-21521" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We're given that $x=4$ is a stationary point of $f(x).$ We will use the second derivative test to classify this stationary point.\n\nCalculating the first derivative for the function $f(x)=-\\frac{{x}^{3}}{2}+24x-36,$ we get\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=-\\frac{3{x}^{2}}{2}+24.\n$$\n\nCalculating the second derivative, we get \n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(x)=-3x.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=4,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(4) & =-3(4) \\\\  & =-12<0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the second derivative is negative, we conclude that $f(x)$ has a relative maximum at $x=4.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14065668/state.json" "file_sha256" "e511109240535f5f98d64c365250a5834ddfbc9fc45b29ff3a8d5c76bc911657"} {"question" "q-21521" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14065668/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "7ad5bf91b4e12d86df59584f1d9f010aa08b83bce64bdb52579a71f9358b936e"} {"question" "q-21521" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14065668/state.json" "file_sha256" "e511109240535f5f98d64c365250a5834ddfbc9fc45b29ff3a8d5c76bc911657"} {"question" "q-21521" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14065668/state.json" "file_sha256" "e511109240535f5f98d64c365250a5834ddfbc9fc45b29ff3a8d5c76bc911657"} {"question" "q-21521" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "III only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14065668/state.json" "file_sha256" "e511109240535f5f98d64c365250a5834ddfbc9fc45b29ff3a8d5c76bc911657"} {"question" "q-22580" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the function $f(x)={e}^{-x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.$ Given that ${f}^{′}(-\\frac{\\pi }{4})=0,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${f}^{″}(-\\frac{\\pi }{4})>0$\n- $f(x)$ has a relative minimum at $x=-\\frac{\\pi }{4}$\n- $f(x)$ has a relative maximum at $x=-\\frac{\\pi }{4}$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14065668/state.json" "file_sha256" "e511109240535f5f98d64c365250a5834ddfbc9fc45b29ff3a8d5c76bc911657"} {"question" "q-22580" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We're given that $x=-\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ is a stationary point of $f(x).$ We will use the second derivative test to classify this stationary point.\n\nCalculating the first derivative for the function $f(x)={e}^{-x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x,$ we get\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=-{e}^{-x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-{e}^{-x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=-{e}^{-x}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x).\n$$\n\nCalculating the second derivative, we get \n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(x)={e}^{-x}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)-{e}^{-x}(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)=2{e}^{-x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=-\\frac{\\pi }{4},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(-\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & =2{e}^{\\pi /4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =2{e}^{\\pi /4}(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}) \\\\  & =-\\sqrt{2}{e}^{\\pi /4}<0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the second derivative is negative, we conclude that $f(x)$ has a relative maximum at $x=-\\frac{\\pi }{4}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14065668/state.json" "file_sha256" "e511109240535f5f98d64c365250a5834ddfbc9fc45b29ff3a8d5c76bc911657"} {"question" "q-22580" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14065668/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "7ad5bf91b4e12d86df59584f1d9f010aa08b83bce64bdb52579a71f9358b936e"} {"question" "q-22580" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14065668/state.json" "file_sha256" "e511109240535f5f98d64c365250a5834ddfbc9fc45b29ff3a8d5c76bc911657"} {"question" "q-22580" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14065668/state.json" "file_sha256" "e511109240535f5f98d64c365250a5834ddfbc9fc45b29ff3a8d5c76bc911657"} {"question" "q-22580" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "III only"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14065668/state.json" "file_sha256" "e511109240535f5f98d64c365250a5834ddfbc9fc45b29ff3a8d5c76bc911657"} {"question" "q-114415" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "For the function $f(x)=(1-x+{x}^{2}){e}^{x},$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $f(x)$ has a relative minimum at $x=0$\n- ${f}^{″}(0)>0$\n- $f(x)$ has a relative maximum at $x=1$\n- $f(x)$ has a relative maximum at $x=-1$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14065668/state.json" "file_sha256" "e511109240535f5f98d64c365250a5834ddfbc9fc45b29ff3a8d5c76bc911657"} {"question" "q-114415" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We need to identify the critical points. Then, we need to classify them using the second derivative test.\n\nFirst, we need to identify the critical points. Differentiating $f(x)=(1-x+{x}^{2}){e}^{x},$ we get\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=(1-x+{x}^{2})\\cdot {e}^{x}+(-1+2x){e}^{x}=({x}^{2}+x){e}^{x},\n$$\n\nand solving ${f}^{′}(x)=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({x}^{2}+x){e}^{x} & =0 \\\\ x(x+1) & =0 \\\\ x & =-1,0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we have critical points at $x=-1,0.$\n\nNext, we need to determine the sign of ${f}^{″}(x)$ at the critical points. Computing ${f}^{″}(x),$ we get\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(x)=({x}^{2}+x)\\cdot {e}^{x}+(2x+1){e}^{x}=({x}^{2}+3x+1){e}^{x},\n$$\n\nand evaluating ${f}^{″}(x)$ at each critical point, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(-1) & =-{e}^{-1}<0 \\\\ {f}^{″}(0) & =1>0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe conclude that:\n\n- Statements I and II are true. The second derivative is positive at $x=0,$ so there is a relative minimum of $f(x).$\n\n- Statements III is false. $x=1$ is not a critical point.\n\n- Statement IV is true.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and IV only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14065668/state.json" "file_sha256" "e511109240535f5f98d64c365250a5834ddfbc9fc45b29ff3a8d5c76bc911657"} {"question" "q-114415" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14065668/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "7ad5bf91b4e12d86df59584f1d9f010aa08b83bce64bdb52579a71f9358b936e"} {"question" "q-114415" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14065668/state.json" "file_sha256" "e511109240535f5f98d64c365250a5834ddfbc9fc45b29ff3a8d5c76bc911657"} {"question" "q-114415" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I, II, and III only"] ["text" "I, II, and IV only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14065668/state.json" "file_sha256" "e511109240535f5f98d64c365250a5834ddfbc9fc45b29ff3a8d5c76bc911657"} {"question" "q-114415" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "I, II, and IV only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14065668/state.json" "file_sha256" "e511109240535f5f98d64c365250a5834ddfbc9fc45b29ff3a8d5c76bc911657"}] "usage" {"q-21500" [] "q-114415" []} "basis" 2176 "retractions" [] "immutable_answers" [17592186293836 17592186293837 17592186293838 17592186293839 17592186293840 17592186303567 17592186303568 17592186303569 17592186303570 17592186303571] "immutable_fields" [[17592186293835 :answer-field/correct] [17592186293835 :answer-field/id] [17592186293835 :answer-field/key] [17592186293835 :answer-field/type] [17592186303566 :answer-field/correct] [17592186303566 :answer-field/id] [17592186303566 :answer-field/key] [17592186303566 :answer-field/type]] "no_history" []}
